    id: "dative",
    title: "To whom and to where: -ಗೆ / -ಕ್ಕೆ",
    blurb: "Say where you are going and who you are giving something to, with the ending -ಗೆ / -ಕ್ಕೆ after the noun.",
    deck: "Getting around",
    body: `
<p>English puts "to" before the noun: to the house, to Ravi. Kannada puts a case ending after the noun: <b>ಮನೆ</b> becomes <b>ಮನೆಗೆ</b> <i>manege</i>, "to the house". This ending, the dative, covers going to a place and giving to a person. You have used it already in <b>ನನಗೆ</b>, "to me".</p>
<h3>One ending, three shapes</h3>
<p>The ending changes with the last sound of the noun, and sometimes a linking sound comes in between.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>The noun ends in</th><th>Ending</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>-ಎ or -ಇ</td><td class="k">ಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಮನೆಗೆ, ಶಾಲೆಗೆ, ರವಿಗೆ</td></tr><tr><td>-ಉ (the ಉ becomes ಇ)</td><td class="k">ಇಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿಗೆ, ಊರಿಗೆ</td></tr><tr><td>-ಅ (things and places)</td><td class="k">ಕ್ಕೆ</td><td class="k">ಕೆಲಸಕ್ಕೆ, ಅಮೆರಿಕಕ್ಕೆ</td></tr><tr><td>-ಅ (people: linking ನ)</td><td class="k">ನಿಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಅಮ್ಮನಿಗೆ, ಅಪ್ಪನಿಗೆ</td></tr><tr><td>a consonant (English loans)</td><td class="k">ಇಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಆಫೀಸಿಗೆ, ಬ್ಯಾಂಕಿಗೆ</td></tr></table></div>
<p>To use it you need an action verb, and the course has not taught those yet. For now, borrow four polite request words; lesson 16 explains them: <b>ಹೋಗಿ</b> <i>hōgi</i> (go), <b>ಬನ್ನಿ</b> <i>banni</i> (come), <b>ಕೊಡಿ</b> <i>koḍi</i> (give) and <b>ಹೇಳಿ</b> <i>hēḷi</i> (tell).</p>
{{ex:ಮನೆಗೆ ಹೋಗಿ.|manege hōgi.|Go home.}}
{{ex:ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿಗೆ ಬನ್ನಿ.|bengaḷūrige banni.|Come to Bengaluru.}}
{{ex:ಅಮ್ಮನಿಗೆ ಕೊಡಿ.|ammanige koḍi.|Give it to Mom.}}
<p>It is just as handy at a ticket counter or a bus stand:</p>
{{ex:ಮೈಸೂರಿಗೆ ಎರಡು ಟಿಕೆಟ್ ಕೊಡಿ.|maisūrige eraḍu ṭikeṭ koḍi.|Two tickets to Mysuru, please.}}
{{ex:ಮೈಸೂರಿಗೆ ಬಸ್ ಎಲ್ಲಿದೆ?|maisūrige bas ellide?|Where's the bus to Mysuru?}}
<h3>Pronouns</h3>
<p>Pronouns change shape before the ending. "To me" is <b>ನನಗೆ</b>, never ನಾನುಗೆ.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Pronoun</th><th>to …</th><th>Pronoun</th><th>to …</th></tr><tr><td class="k">ನಾನು</td><td class="k">ನನಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಅವನು</td><td class="k">ಅವನಿಗೆ</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ನೀನು</td><td class="k">ನಿನಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಅವಳು</td><td class="k">ಅವಳಿಗೆ</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ನೀವು</td><td class="k">ನಿಮಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಅವರು</td><td class="k">ಅವರಿಗೆ</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ನಾವು</td><td class="k">ನಮಗೆ</td><td class="k">ಅದು</td><td class="k">ಅದಕ್ಕೆ</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:ಅವಳಿಗೆ ಹೇಳಿ.|avaḷige hēḷi.|Tell her.}}
<p>The same forms carry the person in the phrases you already know: <b>ನನಗೆ ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಕನ್ನಡ ಬರುತ್ತೆ</b> and <b>ನನಗೆ ಒಬ್ಬ ತಮ್ಮ ಇದ್ದಾನೆ</b>. In the next lesson they do even more work: in Kannada, the one who likes, wants or knows something also takes the dative.</p>
<h3>Where to?</h3>
<p>A question word can take the ending too. <b>ಎಲ್ಲಿ</b> is "where"; with the ending, <b>ಎಲ್ಲಿಗೆ?</b> <i>ellige?</i> asks "where to?". An auto driver may ask you exactly this, and the answer is just the place with its ending.</p>
{{ex:ಎಲ್ಲಿಗೆ? ಆಫೀಸಿಗೆ.|ellige? āfīsige.|Where to? To the office.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> adding -ಗೆ to a noun ending in -ಉ without the linking change. <b>ಬೆಂಗಳೂರುಗೆ</b> → <b>ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿಗೆ</b>. The ಉ turns into ಇ before the ending, so say it the same way for every place ending in -ಉ: <b>ಮೈಸೂರಿಗೆ</b>, <b>ಊರಿಗೆ</b>.</div>
`,
