    blurb: "Say where someone or something is, where it comes from, who you are with, and what is on, under or near what.",
    deck: "Getting around",
    body: `
<p>English uses small words before the noun: <i>in</i> Bengaluru, <i>from</i> Mysuru, <i>with</i> Mom. Kannada works the other way round: a case ending after the noun, or a separate word after it. You know the pattern from -ಗೆ, "to".</p>
<h3>"In, at": -ಅಲ್ಲಿ</h3>
<p>You have been saying <b>ಮನೆಯಲ್ಲಿ</b>, "at home", since lesson 9. It is <b>ಮನೆ</b> plus the ending -ಅಲ್ಲಿ, with a linking ಯ in between. The linking sound depends on the noun, much as with -ಗೆ:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>The noun ends in</th><th>"in, at"</th><th>"from"</th></tr><tr><td>-ಎ or -ಇ</td><td class="k">ಮನೆಯಲ್ಲಿ</td><td class="k">ಮನೆಯಿಂದ</td></tr><tr><td>-ಉ</td><td class="k">ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿನಲ್ಲಿ</td><td class="k">ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿನಿಂದ</td></tr><tr><td>-ಅ</td><td class="k">ಅಮೆರಿಕದಲ್ಲಿ</td><td class="k">ಅಮೆರಿಕದಿಂದ</td></tr><tr><td>a consonant (English loans)</td><td class="k">ಆಫೀಸಿನಲ್ಲಿ</td><td class="k">ಆಫೀಸಿನಿಂದ</td></tr></table></div>
<p>In everyday speech these get shorter: you will hear <b>ಮನೇಲಿ</b> and <b>ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಲ್ಲಿ</b>. Both are fine, and the course accepts them when you type an answer.</p>
{{ex:ನಾನು ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಇದ್ದೀನಿ.|nānu bengaḷūrinalli iddīni.|I'm in Bengaluru. I live in Bengaluru.}}
{{ex:ಅಪ್ಪ ಆಫೀಸಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಇದ್ದಾರೆ.|appa āfīsinalli iddāre.|Dad is at the office.}}
<p>To ask, use <b>ಎಲ್ಲಿ</b>, "where", in the same spot: <b>ಅಮ್ಮ ಎಲ್ಲಿ ಇದ್ದಾರೆ?</b> The answer simply puts the place where the question word was. Keep "at" and "to" apart: <b>ಮನೆಯಲ್ಲಿ</b> is where you are, <b>ಮನೆಗೆ</b> is where you are going.</p>
<h3>"From": -ಇಂದ</h3>
<p>"From" is the ending <b>ಇಂದ</b> <i>inda</i>, with the same linking sounds as -ಅಲ್ಲಿ. To say where a person comes from, though, keep the "person from" word of lesson 10: <b>ನಾನು ಅಮೆರಿಕದವಳು</b>.</p>
{{ex:ಬೆಂಗಳೂರಿನಿಂದ ಮೈಸೂರಿಗೆ ಎರಡು ಟಿಕೆಟ್ ಕೊಡಿ.|bengaḷūrininda maisūrige eraḍu ṭikeṭ koḍi.|Two tickets from Bengaluru to Mysuru, please.}}
<h3>"With", "on", "under", "near"</h3>
<p>These are separate words that come after the noun, and the noun before them takes its "of" form, like <b>ನನ್ನ</b>: <b>ಅಮ್ಮನ ಜೊತೆ</b> <i>ammana jote</i>, "with Mom", literally "Mom's company". The others work the same way: <b>ಮೇಲೆ</b> <i>mēle</i> (on), <b>ಕೆಳಗೆ</b> <i>keḷage</i> (under), <b>ಹತ್ತಿರ</b> <i>hattira</i> (near). <b>ಮೇಜು</b> <i>mēju</i> is a table; its "of" form is <b>ಮೇಜಿನ</b>.</p>
{{ex:ನಾನು ಅಮ್ಮನ ಜೊತೆ ಇದ್ದೀನಿ.|nānu ammana jote iddīni.|I'm with Mom.}}
{{ex:ಪುಸ್ತಕ ಮೇಜಿನ ಮೇಲೆ ಇದೆ.|pustaka mējina mēle ide.|The book is on the table.}}
{{ex:ಬ್ಯಾಂಕ್ ಮನೆಯ ಹತ್ತಿರ ಇದೆ.|byānk maneya hattira ide.|The bank is near the house.}}
<p>In speech the "of" ending is often dropped with things and places: <b>ಮನೆ ಹತ್ತಿರ</b> is as common as <b>ಮನೆಯ ಹತ್ತಿರ</b>. With people, keep it: <b>ಅಮ್ಮನ ಜೊತೆ</b>, <b>ನನ್ನ ಜೊತೆ</b>, "with me".</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> putting "with" first, the English way. <b>ಜೊತೆ ಅಮ್ಮ</b> → <b>ಅಮ್ಮನ ಜೊತೆ</b>. In Kannada the little word always comes after the noun.</div>
`,
